            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Debugging in .NET</h2>
                    <p>Debugging is an essential skill for any developer. .NET provides excellent debugging support in Visual Studio, VS Code, and JetBrains Rider. In this lesson, we'll cover the tools and techniques you need to find and fix bugs efficiently.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Setting Breakpoints</h2>
                    <p>Breakpoints pause execution so you can inspect the state of your application:</p>

                    <div class="debug-tips">
                        <div class="tip">
                            <h4>Regular Breakpoints</h4>
                            <p>Click in the left margin or press F9 to set/remove a breakpoint on a line.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="tip">
                            <h4>Conditional Breakpoints</h4>
                            <p>Right-click a breakpoint to add conditions. Only break when <code>i > 100</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="tip">
                            <h4>Logpoints</h4>
                            <p>Log a message instead of breaking. Great for production-like debugging.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Add a conditional breakpoint here that only breaks when count > 10
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 100; i++)
{
    var count = Process(i); // Set breakpoint on this line
    Console.WriteLine(count);
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Debugger Controls</h2>
                    <p>Once paused at a breakpoint, use these controls:</p>

                    <div class="controls-grid">
                        <div class="control-item">
                            <kbd>F5</kbd>
                            <span>Continue (run until next breakpoint)</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="control-item">
                            <kbd>F10</kbd>
                            <span>Step Over (execute line, don't enter methods)</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="control-item">
                            <kbd>F11</kbd>
                            <span>Step Into (enter the method call)</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="control-item">
                            <kbd>Shift+F11</kbd>
                            <span>Step Out (finish current method, return to caller)</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="control-item">
                            <kbd>Shift+F5</kbd>
                            <span>Stop Debugging</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Inspecting Variables</h2>
                    <p>While debugging, you can inspect variable values:</p>

                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Hover:</strong> Mouse over a variable to see its value</li>
                        <li><strong>Locals Window:</strong> Shows all local variables in current scope</li>
                        <li><strong>Watch Window:</strong> Add specific expressions to monitor</li>
                        <li><strong>Immediate Window:</strong> Execute code and evaluate expressions</li>
                    </ul>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// In the Immediate Window, you can:
// - Evaluate expressions: user.Name
// - Call methods: user.GetFullName()
// - Change values: user.Age = 30
// - Check conditions: user.Age > 18</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Debugging in VS Code</h2>
                    <p>VS Code requires a <code>launch.json</code> configuration:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-json">{
  "version": "0.2.0",
  "configurations": [
    {
      "name": ".NET Core Launch (console)",
      "type": "coreclr",
      "request": "launch",
      "preLaunchTask": "build",
      "program": "${workspaceFolder}/bin/Debug/net8.0/MyApp.dll",
      "args": [],
      "cwd": "${workspaceFolder}",
      "console": "integratedTerminal",
      "stopAtEntry": false
    }
  ]
}</code></pre>

                    <p>Press F5 or use the Run and Debug panel to start debugging.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Logging</h2>
                    <p>Strategic logging helps you understand what your application is doing:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

public class UserService
{
    private readonly ILogger&lt;UserService&gt; _logger;
    
    public UserService(ILogger&lt;UserService&gt; logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }
    
    public async Task&lt;User?&gt; GetUserAsync(int id)
    {
        _logger.LogDebug("Getting user with ID: {UserId}", id);
        
        try
        {
            var user = await _repository.FindAsync(id);
            
            if (user == null)
            {
                _logger.LogWarning("User not found: {UserId}", id);
                return null;
            }
            
            _logger.LogInformation("Found user: {UserName}", user.Name);
            return user;
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            _logger.LogError(ex, "Error getting user {UserId}", id);
            throw;
        }
    }
}</code></pre>

                    <h3>Log Levels</h3>
                    <div class="log-levels">
                        <div class="level"><code>Trace</code> - Most detailed, for diagnostic purposes</div>
                        <div class="level"><code>Debug</code> - Debugging information</div>
                        <div class="level"><code>Information</code> - General flow of application</div>
                        <div class="level"><code>Warning</code> - Abnormal or unexpected events</div>
                        <div class="level"><code>Error</code> - Errors and exceptions</div>
                        <div class="level"><code>Critical</code> - Failures requiring immediate attention</div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Common Exceptions</h2>
                    <p>Learn to recognize and fix these common exceptions:</p>

                    <div class="exception-grid">
                        <div class="exception-card">
                            <h4>NullReferenceException</h4>
                            <p>You tried to use a null object reference.</p>
                            <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Fix: Check for null
if (user != null)
{
    Console.WriteLine(user.Name);
}
// Or use null-conditional
Console.WriteLine(user?.Name);</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="exception-card">
                            <h4>ArgumentNullException</h4>
                            <p>A null argument was passed to a method that doesn't accept it.</p>
                            <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Fix: Validate arguments
public void Process(string data)
{
    ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(data);
    // ... process data
}</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="exception-card">
                            <h4>InvalidOperationException</h4>
                            <p>The object is in an invalid state for the operation.</p>
                            <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Common: Empty collection
var first = list.First(); // Throws if empty
var first = list.FirstOrDefault(); // Returns null/default</code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Using Debug.Assert</h2>
                    <p>Assertions help catch programming errors during development:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">using System.Diagnostics;

public void ProcessOrder(Order order)
{
    // These only run in Debug builds
    Debug.Assert(order != null, "Order cannot be null");
    Debug.Assert(order.Items.Count > 0, "Order must have items");
    Debug.Assert(order.Total >= 0, "Total cannot be negative");
    
    // Process the order...
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Analyzing Stack Traces</h2>
                    <p>Stack traces show you exactly where an exception occurred:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash">Unhandled exception. System.NullReferenceException: Object reference not set
   at MyApp.Services.UserService.GetUser(Int32 id) in UserService.cs:line 25
   at MyApp.Controllers.UsersController.Get(Int32 id) in UsersController.cs:line 18
   at Program.Main(String[] args) in Program.cs:line 10</code></pre>

                    <p>Read from bottom to top to understand the call chain. The top line shows where the error occurred.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Diagnostic Tools</h2>
                    
                    <h3>dotnet-counters</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Install
dotnet tool install --global dotnet-counters

# Monitor your app
dotnet-counters monitor --process-id 12345</code></pre>

                    <h3>dotnet-trace</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Collect a trace
dotnet tool install --global dotnet-trace
dotnet-trace collect --process-id 12345</code></pre>

                    <h3>dotnet-dump</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Capture a memory dump
dotnet tool install --global dotnet-dump
dotnet-dump collect --process-id 12345</code></pre>
                </section>

